course: dictionary
lesson: 9178
title: Soak and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The same word can mean different things, and the context tells you which meaning is right. «Leave the beans to soak overnight» means leave them in water until they are wet all through, which is what cooks do.
explain: «The sponge soaks up the spilt juice» means the sponge takes the liquid in, just as a paper towel does. Soak can also mean to enjoy or learn a lot of something, as in «She wanted to soak up the sunshine» or «He soaked up knowledge all year».
explain: Ask what fits in the sentence. If you were soaked in the rain, you would be wet all through, so soaked means completely wet. Try your meaning in the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: To soak something is to leave it in water or another liquid until it becomes completely wet.
fact: «Soak up» means to take in a liquid, as a sponge or paper towel does.
fact: «Soaking wet» or «soaked through» means wet all the way through.
fact: «A soak» is a long time spent in water, like a long bath.
fact: Absorbent materials, like sponge, paper and cloth, soak up water; waterproof materials, like plastic and rubber, do not.
exercise: «Paper towel can soak up spilt milk in seconds.» What does soak up mean here? | Pour the liquid out | *Take the liquid in | Throw the liquid away | Warm the liquid up
exercise: Dad says, «I got soaking wet on the walk home!» What does soaking wet mean? | *Wet all the way through | Shiny like glass | Cold but dry | Slightly sticky
exercise: Why is a plastic bag called waterproof? | It is full of holes | It soaks up water like a sponge | *Water cannot get through it | It is made of paper
test: «Leave the beans to soak overnight.» What does soak mean here? | Throw them in the bin | *Leave them in water until they are wet | Cook them quickly in a pan | Dry them in the sun
test: «She wanted to soak up the sunshine all summer.» What does soak up mean here? | *Enjoy a lot of | Melt away | Hide from | Wrap up in
exam: Which word means a long time spent in water, like a long bath? | *Soak | Puddle | Splash | Drip
